\langle 2x, x^2, 1\\rangle$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552430 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d2b2827-fc94-5958-9a7b-16367fc19078" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551698} {:answer/id #uuid "5bdc41c8-37d7-56e6-9cda-59976d63909e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551887} {:answer/id #uuid "6c06907e-68fd-5846-846e-61d3ebbdcfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551915} {:answer/id #uuid "82ba78bb-4833-5028-9cf9-72e3cb59f32f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551975} {:answer/id #uuid "e9e5bb54-585e-59f5-8426-0a44c3083849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9e5bb54-585e-59f5-8426-0a44c3083849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552213} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ae6c02-1c78-5b5d-926b-2d33957e08c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4696ec15-27d3-55f9-a25e-60bb43e6e055" :question/math-academy-id "q-127075" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{3}\\,i+(2+x)\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{3}=8{sin}^{3}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =2+x=2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,du=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(8{sin}^{3}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(2+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }16{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{0}16{u}^{3}+16u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }+0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552492 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d243fe-78bb-52be-a2f0-f9815804e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551859} {:answer/id #uuid "62fe97bc-579e-5ca3-9781-e68777e97322" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551899} {:answer/id #uuid "86c104da-5667-58a2-96d7-fb45fe8f70c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551990} {:answer/id #uuid "cc321452-9e74-5980-b3a3-7f6a5738db5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552153} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bc6932-c933-563c-a5c7-9780b5ef00c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552234}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d243fe-78bb-52be-a2f0-f9815804e4d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551859} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe3669d-5fb8-5cf8-8402-eaff7b7d0308" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2316759-1f4d-59b3-aebb-24c5b1ac8267" :question/math-academy-id "q-127061" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)={y}^{2}\\,i-xy\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & ={y}^{2}=4{sin}^{2}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-xy=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the substitution $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=u,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt=du,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4{sin}^{2}⁡t)\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }8{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{0}6{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d42fa31-693c-5e63-8b30-f714f163cf61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551774} {:answer/id #uuid "5a97f892-a6ec-5102-a2f8-b11ba307ea5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551880} {:answer/id #uuid "788d4fd5-7983-5bde-ac02-62cb31dfdb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551948} {:answer/id #uuid "7bd545ee-d36f-5cc5-9368-52d6c0d580da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} {:answer/id #uuid "ceda2cc6-35df-59c0-b345-80992bffb35d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552157}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bd545ee-d36f-5cc5-9368-52d6c0d580da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551960} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1fc0cd0-57f3-5df9-9d82-31c607584763" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c696854b-d1cc-50f5-8398-3fba96da4739" :question/math-academy-id "q-127063" :question/problem "Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=xi+\\frac{1}{4}\\,j$ across the closed curve $C$ given by $r(t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field and $C$ is a piecewise-smooth, simple, closed curve, then the flux of $F$ across $C$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ denotes the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C.$ \n\nAlong the curve $C,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =x=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the integral using the double-angle formula for cosine, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot (8\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)-\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32{cos}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }32\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+16t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =(0+32\\pi -1)-(0-1) \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552753 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b0e6ff-0fbd-5bdd-a351-5887ff0ce816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552755} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9669fe-7bf9-55db-8767-162819a1a311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552757} {:answer/id #uuid "80c335df-1961-5907-bf45-3069a1f3a20c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552764} {:answer/id #uuid "b5e9081b-d311-5781-aebc-80c0bc07299b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552768} {:answer/id #uuid "cf5889ad-17bf-5a3d-b6a8-b354a97102a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9669fe-7bf9-55db-8767-162819a1a311" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552757} :answer-field/id #uuid "7183f683-d36a-5198-94c6-00ee975122f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f2bf46b-da63-57bb-88ab-6be57fcad23d" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-1,3,-5⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-4,4⟩,$ and $c=⟨-1,3,-5⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -4 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-4 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-4)\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 3)i-(2\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 3-(-4)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =8i+6j+2k \\\\  & =⟨8,6,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-1,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨8,6,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 8+3\\cdot 6+(-5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552766 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ca04c36-b5a0-50ad-a1b0-c6c4307ea445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552756} {:answer/id #uuid "4d94f0d4-c89c-590f-80d6-5211dc248051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "46" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552758} {:answer/id #uuid "64f691aa-9638-5417-ab68-e752ecd5cc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552760} {:answer/id #uuid "cedecffc-9f77-54b1-a175-99d63c0ce5d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552772} {:answer/id #uuid "d0e1e523-9968-5353-aa90-7d693a6d8352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64f691aa-9638-5417-ab68-e752ecd5cc64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552760} :answer-field/id #uuid "9678d55a-9874-5a7a-9a1a-dfdf37ee8c90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552767}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e17b30a-856b-5ad3-9fe4-036dbdb66eb5" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨4,-3,-1⟩,$ $b=⟨-1,-5,-2⟩,$ and $c=⟨-2,-3,-3⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & -5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-5 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -5 \\\\ -2 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-5)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-3))i-((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-2)\\cdot (-2))j+((-1)\\cdot (-3)-(-5)\\cdot (-2))k \\\\  & =9i+j-7k \\\\  & =⟨9,1,-7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,-3,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨9,1,-7⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 9+(-3)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-7) \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "57142571-02b8-5cb5-9f03-3f475fb3243d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552759} {:answer/id #uuid "757c94c4-853f-5f64-a6ad-c4348b0c6d3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552762} {:answer/id #uuid "7c0be516-847e-5063-8396-cf999c8d768b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552763} {:answer/id #uuid "837985ae-de1f-5e35-b1fc-815462b6bcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552765} {:answer/id #uuid "feafabc1-7870-55b4-99a5-8bd34394f8a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "837985ae-de1f-5e35-b1fc-815462b6bcae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552765} :answer-field/id #uuid "007d847d-6ae6-52b6-a497-cf60fa91c74a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552751}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bc421905-ccc1-5356-85d1-164c6440e5a1" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-2,3,-5⟩,$ $b=⟨2,-3,5⟩,$ and $c=⟨-1,1,-2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot 1)i-(2\\cdot (-2)-5\\cdot (-1))j+(2\\cdot 1-(-3)\\cdot (-1))k \\\\  & =i-j-k \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-2,3,-5⟩\\cdot ⟨1,-1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot (-1)+(-5)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba2f35f2-876c-5534-b910-4f67ae6e1e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2f35f2-876c-5534-b910-4f67ae6e1e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552769} :answer-field/id #uuid "c817e349-9d43-5ed3-b1cd-8fc887b45b71" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552771}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d6f58672-c2d9-5b4b-a1ac-70b06c5419c6" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨4,2,-4⟩,$ $b=⟨-3,-1,-4⟩,$ and $c=⟨3,5,-4⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & -1 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}-3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot (-4)-(-4)\\cdot 5)i-((-3)\\cdot (-4)-3\\cdot (-4))j+((-3)\\cdot 5-(-1)\\cdot 3)k \\\\  & =24i-24j-12k \\\\  & =⟨24,-24,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨4,2,-4⟩\\cdot ⟨24,-24,-12⟩ \\\\  & =4\\cdot 24+2\\cdot (-24)+(-4)\\cdot (-12) \\\\  & =96-48+48 \\\\  & =96\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552776 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "243dab41-484b-5283-9e99-e10e0aa94a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "243dab41-484b-5283-9e99-e10e0aa94a0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552754} :answer-field/id #uuid "0542d953-14f3-53d6-b085-2643c62fb607" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e37f57a2-326b-5f48-88f5-0e74cb3e0632" :question/problem "Given that $a=⟨-5,-5,1⟩,$ $b=⟨1,-1,-3⟩,$ and $c=⟨5,-3,2⟩,$ find $a\\cdot (b\\times c)$ using the definition of the scalar triple product.\n\n$a\\cdot (b\\times c)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b\\times c & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{vmatrix}i-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 2\\end{vmatrix}j+\\begin{vmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{vmatrix}k \\\\  & =((-1)\\cdot 2-(-3)\\cdot (-3))i-(1\\cdot 2-5\\cdot (-3))j+(1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 5)k \\\\  & =-11i-17j+2k \\\\  & =⟨-11,-17,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the scalar triple product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot (b\\times c) & =⟨-5,-5,1⟩\\cdot ⟨-11,-17,2⟩ \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-11)+(-5)\\cdot (-17)+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55+85+2 \\\\  & =142\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a1689b3-c36c-5147-a643-ad05385c39c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552946} {:answer/id #uuid "2d67fef5-d17a-5044-86dd-9b0dd6104d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552954} {:answer/id #uuid "513e98ed-26b8-5fdd-95e1-91a5c0e76825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553043} {:answer/id #uuid "a031cdff-6a41-52e9-aeab-5a6e16f24cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{x}^{2}+xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553192} {:answer/id #uuid "d69014c4-c493-5bef-9f1c-7c17c3e7aba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({x}^{2}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553300}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "513e98ed-26b8-5fdd-95e1-91a5c0e76825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553043} :answer-field/id #uuid "7efa0a56-60d9-584e-8007-e5d380ed9d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "30b095cd-424c-553d-bb7d-82a5c5e826eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-127225" :question/problem "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{3}+{y}^{2},\\,{x}^{2}y⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(1,0),(0,2),$ and $(-1,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/84/848dfedddea9803dcf39b4624f2966b63f080920ed19b875dc691e8fe9d3b038.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=4{x}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553576 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21cf8068-05ed-55e2-ac2c-5cbee642e5eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}3{x}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552928} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd0d3f5-7b2e-5d2b-abac-49bdcc1aa2cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553033} {:answer/id #uuid "585d8ed2-ddec-5562-bde8-6c36dfbec00c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553058} {:answer/id #uuid "de294dc0-1a3f-5566-a574-17c08dcba154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{y}^{2}(-{y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553310} {:answer/id #uuid "fe53b468-ca8f-540a-b5a9-6cfac0008d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe53b468-ca8f-540a-b5a9-6cfac0008d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553372} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bc52748-240b-5997-9e36-b5b9d7c36232" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553440}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5c8c9fb-a926-5fe7-b24d-28d3915fc2cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-127230" :question/problem "Consider the rectangle $C$ with vertices $(-1,0),(-1,-2),(3,-2),$ and $(3,0),$ traversed counterclockwise. Using the flux form of Green's theorem, express the flux of the vector field\n$F(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}\\,i-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,j$\nthrough $C$ as a repeated integral." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e1/e1e4386f3cc75afb63823d74daa27b517e8e47d51fa08c5c9e5bf3ca6f3ca88c.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{6}$ and $Q(x,y)=-{x}^{4}{y}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{6},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-2\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(3{x}^{2}{y}^{6}-3{y}^{2}{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-2}^{0}3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}({y}^{4}-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553757 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "591a446f-82dd-564c-b15f-8e8267e36fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553758} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7ef8e8-03eb-5e71-9954-b61f0cd0045d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553759} {:answer/id #uuid "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553763} {:answer/id #uuid "aab019ae-93c7-50b3-9811-d455898d1942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553765} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2ac1da-5902-5d50-a8d4-5ac0daceec12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996ebf57-fd88-5775-876c-167c46de26ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553763} :answer-field/id #uuid "779da836-a9bf-5772-a9db-115da484b79e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "391564c7-a751-5991-b788-dcbb08b27c2c" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 2 x^{2} + 4 y, \\; 2 x^{2} + 4 x y \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 y$ and $Q(x,y)=2 x^{2} + 4 x y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=4 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=4 x.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{2}}\\left(8 x\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[8 x y\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{2}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(8 x^{3}\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{4}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553764 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "193c7f3f-1b13-52cf-b99e-2cb82e2ec402" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553755} {:answer/id #uuid "30eb9b0a-3b5c-550c-97df-45c92636de58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553756} {:answer/id #uuid "75dd0d96-1827-5799-ba11-bbee875c6ba2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553760} {:answer/id #uuid "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553762} {:answer/id #uuid "d900e075-bbea-5147-aeda-64c204455c22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553767}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84167764-9866-593d-9f3f-728b74e99429" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553762} :answer-field/id #uuid "05ec21ed-67a9-5f57-8257-9837d31fa900" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f300428-dd28-5d22-81b0-c450650df73d" :question/problem "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left\\langle 28 x y, \\; 12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/e6/e6f6a5cf9b37fe16f17fe74face946d85b426badf8db710ca4a7ea308a1ff2ec.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=28 x y$ and $Q(x,y)=12 x^{2} y + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}=28 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}=12 x^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\leq x\\leq 1,\\quad 0\\leq y\\leq x^{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\oint_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds&=\\oint_C P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial x}+\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\iint_D\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dA \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{0}^{x^{3}}\\left(12 x^{2} + 28 y\\right)\\,dy\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left[12 x^{2} y + 14 y^{2}\\right]_{y=0}^{y=x^{3}}\\,dx \\\\&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(x^{5} \\left(14 x + 12\\right)\\right)\\,dx \\\\&=\\left[2 x^{7} + 2 x^{6}\\right]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\&=\\left(4\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\&=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553809 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e91180b-6331-59e6-b109-17873fdcd979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+18=\\frac{2}{17}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553814} {:answer/id #uuid "54fcf7fa-29a1-50a6-8a2b-f8e763435745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553819} {:answer/id #uuid "598ab248-3cae-524c-9fc7-76f8b1fd9414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553821} {:answer/id #uuid "5f42491a-9739-5bf3-8c90-e0807021a26c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553822} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b29cff-3a49-5f0b-b906-a35336418455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+18=-\\frac{17}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "598ab248-3cae-524c-9fc7-76f8b1fd9414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553821} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d58c371-70ac-58e2-a830-97e519cbae19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553827}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "156d3ad5-139f-5ce1-be31-d15bc1d7bc46" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+5,\\qquad y=t^{3}+5 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+5=-4+5=1 \\\\ y & =(-2)^{3}+5 (-2)=-8-10=-18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,-18).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=3 t^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{3 t^{2}+5}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{3 (-2)^{2}+5}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}=\\frac{17}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(1,-18)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{17}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+18=\\frac{17}{2}(x-1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553823 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02127cc3-5af8-5028-af65-03c30e9fe0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12=\\frac{1}{11}(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553806} {:answer/id #uuid "047526f7-0fc2-546c-96a9-bb524e5db98c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12=-11(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553807} {:answer/id #uuid "42a51268-246f-58ac-af6a-cf3f33fa395d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+12=11(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553815} {:answer/id #uuid "5205a794-1a72-5a43-aeec-856be7dfeb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12=11(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553817} {:answer/id #uuid "d42a96db-021e-5302-939c-143e73df7442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12=11(x+9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553837}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5205a794-1a72-5a43-aeec-856be7dfeb5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-12=11(x-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553817} :answer-field/id #uuid "0ab7b7c5-34e3-5ad2-846b-83d04db15199" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553808}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "631a8bbe-eb86-5a24-9088-75cad1180129" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=-2 t+5,\\qquad y=-2 t^{3}+2 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2 (-2)+5=4+5=9 \\\\ y & =-2 (-2)^{3}+2 (-2)=16-4=12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(9,12).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=-2,\\qquad y'(t)=-6 t^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{-6 t^{2}+2}{-2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{-6 (-2)^{2}+2}{-2}=\\frac{-22}{-2}=11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(9,12)$ and the slope $m=11.$ We get\n\n$$\ny-12=11(x-9).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "284cc5f1-30ca-5d6d-8545-526f35b4066d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22=-\\frac{27}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553810} {:answer/id #uuid "381d922a-2cd7-5c37-84dd-ff625781507d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553813} {:answer/id #uuid "8a92aeb3-1beb-54cc-b477-c93cbea1b51d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553826} {:answer/id #uuid "9ddfe5e6-9612-5610-90a7-9b6636d68468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22=\\frac{2}{27}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553829} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c5a94f-f27d-514a-ac07-2b56a3d51dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4c5a94f-f27d-514a-ac07-2b56a3d51dc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553831} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7ce7cf3-8ac9-5f5b-8f37-c3976de623e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8479a10a-7f66-540d-8458-dda374fac829" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2 t+1,\\qquad y=2 t^{3}+3 t,\\qquad -\\infty<t<\\infty.\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=-2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2 (-2)+1=-4+1=-3 \\\\ y & =2 (-2)^{3}+3 (-2)=-16-6=-22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(-3,-22).$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2,\\qquad y'(t)=6 t^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}=\\frac{6 t^{2}+3}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{6 (-2)^{2}+3}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}=\\frac{27}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-3,-22)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{27}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny+22=\\frac{27}{2}(x+3).\n$$"}]}
